00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:26.040
versus the NASDAQ are not available in today's briefing. Now, here's something that most retail

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:30.980
investors get wrong about dividends and it can quietly erode returns if ignored.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:37.920
Imagine you bought BCE yesterday expecting to collect the upcoming quarterly dividend. Your

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:44.560
order filled at the closing price but the stock opened today roughly 1.5% lower because buyers

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:50.880
after the X dividend date no longer receive that payment. The mechanism works through the exchanges

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:56.780
automatic adjustment where the previous day's close becomes today's opening print minus the dividend

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:04.220
amount. The actual gap can sometimes widen or narrow due to normal trading. Most retail investors

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:09.760
don't realize the adjustment happens before the market opens and can be masked by broader moves.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.840
Professionals always check the X dividend date on the company's investor page and compare it

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.860
against their settlement cycle before placing the order. The biggest mistake with dividend

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:27.740
capture is ignoring the price drop and transaction costs. Instead, always calculate the net yield

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:34.160
after the X date adjustment and only proceed if the annualized return still exceeds your opportunity

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:42.460
cost. Before we wrap, watch for any volume spike above the 20-day average on BCE that could trigger the

